;; cljg.process — streaming subprocess spawn (ADR 0101). Where cljg.io's
;; exec/sh/sh! run a command to completion and buffer its output, `spawn` keeps
;; the process LIVE and hands back live pipes, so the caller streams into and
;; out of it a line/chunk at a time (the cat / less / ffmpeg / long-running
;; child shape):
;;
;;   (require '[cljg.process :as proc] '[cljg.stream :as st])
;;   (let [p (proc/spawn ["cat"])]
;;     (st/write-line (:in p) "hello")   ; write to the child's stdin
;;     (st/close (:in p))                ; EOF → cat drains and exits
;;     (println (st/read-line (:out p))) ; read the child's stdout  => hello
;;     ((:wait p)))                      ; block for exit, => 0
;;
;; The handle is {:in <writable> :out <readable> :err <readable>
;;                :wait (fn [] -> exit-code) :kill (fn [] -> nil)
;;                :alive? (fn [] -> boolean)
;;                :exit-code (fn [] -> int or nil)}.
;; :in / :out / :err are cljg.stream handles (require cljg.stream to read/write
;; them); :wait blocks until the process exits and returns its exit code (a
;; non-zero exit is a normal result); :kill force-terminates it; :exit-code is
;; the non-blocking counterpart of :wait — the exit status once the child has
;; been REAPED, nil before that. It distinguishes "exited with status N" from
;; "not yet reaped" without blocking, which a closed stdout alone cannot.
;;
;; READ THAT PRECISELY: nil means "no status recorded YET", NOT "still
;; running". Those differ by a real window. A child's stdout reaching EOF and
;; the reaper recording its status are separate events, and EOF happens first
;; — so :exit-code read immediately after draining :out returns nil for a
;; process that has already exited. Measured on `sh -c 'echo hi >&2; exit 3'`:
;; nil on 5 runs of 5 immediately at EOF, the correct 3 on all 5 after 250ms.
;;
;; So DO NOT write (if ((:exit-code p)) …) to mean "has it finished" — that
;; is the idiom this docstring used to recommend, and it is wrong at exactly
;; the moment callers reach for it. After draining the streams, call :wait:
;; it returns as soon as the reaper has the status (microseconds once the
;; child is gone) and cannot report nil. Use :exit-code for polling a child
;; you are NOT draining to EOF. :alive? is a
;; NON-BLOCKING liveness check (issue #173) — false once the child has
;; exited, whether or not anything ever called :wait or :kill.
;;
;; The Go half (pkg/bri/proc_spawn.go) is a thin shim over os/exec's
;; StdinPipe/StdoutPipe/StderrPipe; the pipes are wrapped as the same
;; Readable/Writable handles cljg.stream and cljg.net.http use (ONE stream
;; abstraction — spike s56). A single background goroutine owns cmd.Wait()
;; from the moment the process starts (os/exec forbids calling Wait twice),
;; so :wait / :alive? both just read its result — no caller-side reaper is
;; needed, unlike before this fix. Everything here is portable Clojure, so it
;; runs identically interpreted and AOT-compiled. Private Go shims interned
;; before this loads: -proc-spawn -proc-wait -proc-kill -proc-alive?
;; -proc-exit-code.
;;
;; (cljg.io's run-to-completion exec/sh/sh! stay in cljg.io — ADR 0101 makes
;; cljg.process the home for the STREAMING capability; folding exec/sh here is a
;; documented, deferred consolidation, not a half-move.)
;;
;; Loaded as an embedded namespace (ADR 0041/0087): NO (ns …) form — the
;; fully-qualified in-ns + refer open the file.
(clojure.core/in-ns 'cljg.process)
(clojure.core/refer 'clojure.core)

(defn spawn
  "Start a subprocess and return a live handle:
    {:in <writable> :out <readable> :err <readable>
     :wait (fn [] -> exit-code) :kill (fn [] -> nil)
     :alive? (fn [] -> boolean)}
  `command` is a vector [cmd arg…]. opts (optional):
    :env {name value}  merged onto the current environment
    :dir path          the working directory
  The child runs concurrently; stream into :in and out of :out/:err with
  cljg.stream, then call :wait (blocks for exit, returns the exit code) or
  :kill. :alive? never blocks — it answers false once the child has exited,
  whether or not :wait or :kill was ever called, and is safe to call any
  number of times alongside :wait.
  :exit-code never blocks either, and returns nil until the child has been
  REAPED — which is NOT the same instant it exits. Draining :out to EOF then
  reading :exit-code returns nil for an already-exited child (measured: 5 of
  5 runs). To get the status after draining, call :wait; it returns
  immediately once the child is gone. Reach for :exit-code when polling a
  child you are not draining.
  A missing/unrunnable binary throws (it never started)."
  ([command] (spawn command {}))
  ([command opts]
   (let [{:keys [in out err -handle]} (-proc-spawn (vec command) opts)]
     {:in     in
      :out    out
      :err    err
      :wait   (fn [] (-proc-wait -handle))
      :kill   (fn [] (-proc-kill -handle))
      :alive? (fn [] (-proc-alive? -handle))
      :exit-code (fn [] (-proc-exit-code -handle))})))
